<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vlorë</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Valona, Avlonya, Aulon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vlorë, historically Valona, is a port city in south-western Albania on the Bay of Vlorë. Ancient Aulon, it was contested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, the Normans, the Serbian Empire and local lords before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1417. On 28 November 1912 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-qemali">Ismail Qemali</a> proclaimed Albania's independence there, and it served as the first capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vlore.jpg" alt="Vlorë" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vlorari · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qyteti_I_Vlores_Naten_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vlorë lies on the Bay of Vlorë in south-western Albania, on the eastern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-otranto">Strait of Otranto</a>, surrounded by the foothills of the Ceraunian Mountains. The bay forms a natural harbour, which gave the site a significant place in antiquity as a base for trade. The name comes from the ancient Greek Aulon, meaning channel or glen, first recorded in the 2nd century CE by Lucian and Ptolemy. The Albanian form Vlorë developed by a sound change, rhotacism, from Vlonë, while the Italian Valona and the Turkish Avlonya come from the Greek name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The coast of Vlorë was among the Illyrian sites with pre-urban activity from the 11th to 10th centuries BCE, and Greek colonists are traditionally believed to have founded Orikos, Thronion and Aulon on these shores. A large fortified port town, inhabited from the 6th century BCE to the 2nd century CE, lies partly submerged at Triport to the north-west; it may have been moved to the site of the modern city. Aulon was an important port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, in the province of Epirus Nova, and became an episcopal see in the 5th century.</p><p>In the 11th and 12th centuries Vlorë figured in the conflicts between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the Norman Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. A Venetian fleet attacked it in 1321, and from 1346 to 1417 it was the capital of the Principality of Valona, at first a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Serbian Empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> captured it in 1417; Albanian rebels freed it in 1432, but it returned to Ottoman rule and became a sanjak centre under the name Avlonya. Venice held it briefly from 1690 to 1691. A Jewish community, settled with Ottoman support from 1426 and enlarged by exiles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> after 1492, numbered some 2,600 people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> of 1506 and 1520, and traded between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Ottoman cities.</p><p>On 28 November 1912, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-qemali">Ismail Qemali</a> headed the Albanian Declaration of Independence at Vlorë, and the city became Albania's first capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> occupied it in 1914 and again in 1939, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>'s communist government after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the port was leased to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as a submarine base until the break of 1961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vlorë is the third most populous city of Albania and the seat of Vlorë County. It is a major seaport and commercial centre, and its Museum of Independence stands in the 19th-century building where the founders of Albania signed the declaration. The Castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanine">Kaninë</a> and the archaeological sites of Amantia and Oricum lie in the municipality. The city belongs to the cultural region of Labëria, known for polyphonic music that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has recognised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vlorë” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In <b>Vlorë</b>, Ismail Qemali raised the Albanian flag and proclaimed independence from the Ottoman Empire during the First Balkan War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a first assault on the heavily fortified, moated town of Saint-Pierre-le-Moûtier failed, Joan of Arc launched a second that, according to her bodyguard Jean d'<b>Aulon</b>, met little resistance, and the town was taken.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-4">4 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Stefan Dušan of the Serbian Empire took <b>Valona</b> (Vlorë) and Kanina from Byzantium around 1345.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-valona">Principality of Valona</a></li><li><blockquote>During the First Balkan War, as Serbian, Montenegrin and Greek armies overran the Ottoman Balkans, Albanian delegates gathered in <b>Vlorë</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-albania">Provisional Government of Albania</a></li><li><blockquote>Balša II took <b>Valona</b> and Durrës, but died fighting the Ottomans at Savra in 1385.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a></li></ul></section>
